= Research

My research focuses on experimental particle physics, especially dark matter direct detection, neutrino physics, detector calibration, and data analysis. I am also interested in computational methods that connect simulation with the interpretation of experimental data.

== Current Research

=== XENONnT — Detector Response and Energy Calibration

*Area:* Dark matter direct detection / detector physics

This project studies how scintillation and ionization signals are translated into a calibrated energy measurement in the XENONnT detector. It focuses on the light and charge gains, electron extraction, and energy resolution across different science runs.

*Methods:* Calibration lines, detector-response modeling, statistical fitting, systematic cross-checks.

=== Solar pp Neutrino — Reconstruction and Analysis-Cut Efficiency

*Area:* Low-energy electronic recoils / efficiency modeling

This project investigates the probability that a simulated low-energy interaction is reconstructed and survives analysis cuts after its waveform is embedded in realistic detector backgrounds.

*Methods:* Fuse simulation, Saltax waveform injection, straxen reconstruction, truth matching, energy-dependent efficiency estimates.

== Additional Research Interests

- Neutrino detection and rare-event searches
- Monte Carlo simulation and uncertainty quantification
- Detector signal processing and reconstruction
- Computational statistical physics and phase transitions

